From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 18:20:19 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 16/55] channels: arm the input fd only when it can be read

The client and server could spin at full CPU during bulk transfers. Reading a
channel's input is armed in channel_pre_open() with sshbuf_check_reserve(),
but channel_handle_rfd() only reads when sshbuf_avail() is non-zero, and for
channel input buffers sshbuf_avail() keeps about 1/21 of the buffer as
headroom that sshbuf_check_reserve() knows nothing about. While the buffer
sat in that headroom, ppoll() returned at once because the input was
readable, the read was skipped, and the loop repeated until the send side
drained the buffer.

Observed with the 16MiB channel input buffer: len(input)=15978301, which is
16777216 - 16777216/21, avail=0 and io_want set. A 96MB transfer made about
85000 ppoll calls against about 4600 for OpenSSH, and the client used about
a full core for the whole transfer.

Arm the read with the same test the read uses, in channel_pre_open() and in
channel_pre_mux_client(). Over 50ms and 100ms paths the client now uses 1.8
to 3.3 CPU-seconds per GB (medians over 3 runs), against 19 and 37 before,
with unchanged throughput.

Changelog: sphynx 0.3.4

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 channels.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/channels.c b/channels.c
index f59efcd9a..61df54fe3 100644
--- a/channels.c
+++ b/channels.c
@@ -1457,6 +1457,24 @@ channel_tcpwinsz(struct ssh *ssh)
 	return (tcpwinsz);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Can the channel read more into c->input?
+ *
+ * This has to agree with channel_handle_rfd(), which only reads when
+ * sshbuf_avail() is non-zero. For channel input buffers sshbuf_avail() keeps
+ * headroom (about 1/21 of the buffer) that sshbuf_check_reserve() knows
+ * nothing about. Checking only sshbuf_check_reserve() therefore arms the poll
+ * for a read that then does nothing when the buffer is in that headroom:
+ * ppoll() returns at once because the fd is readable, the read is skipped,
+ * and the loop spins at full CPU until the output side drains the buffer.
+ */
+static int
+channel_input_can_read(Channel *c)
+{
+	return sshbuf_check_reserve(c->input, CHAN_RBUF) == 0 &&
+	    sshbuf_avail(c->input) > 0;
+}
+
 static void
 channel_pre_open(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 {
@@ -1464,7 +1482,7 @@ channel_pre_open(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 	if (c->istate == CHAN_INPUT_OPEN &&
 	    c->remote_window > 0 &&
 	    sshbuf_len(c->input) < c->remote_window &&
-	    sshbuf_check_reserve(c->input, CHAN_RBUF) == 0)
+	    channel_input_can_read(c))
 		c->io_want |= SSH_CHAN_IO_RFD;
 	if (c->ostate == CHAN_OUTPUT_OPEN ||
 	    c->ostate == CHAN_OUTPUT_WAIT_DRAIN) {
@@ -1626,7 +1644,7 @@ channel_pre_mux_client(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 {
 	c->io_want = 0;
 	if (c->istate == CHAN_INPUT_OPEN && !c->mux_pause &&
-	    sshbuf_check_reserve(c->input, CHAN_RBUF) == 0)
+	    channel_input_can_read(c))
 		c->io_want |= SSH_CHAN_IO_RFD;
 	if (c->istate == CHAN_INPUT_WAIT_DRAIN) {
 		/* clear buffer immediately (discard any partial packet) */
